🔹 Operators & Expressions

Membership Operators

Membership operators are used to check whether a value exists in a sequence or collection.

- "in"
- "not in"

Identity Operators

Identity operators are used to check whether two references point to the same object.

- "is"
- "is not"

Operator Precedence

Operator precedence determines the order in which operators are evaluated in an expression.

Understanding precedence helps predict how Python evaluates complex expressions.

Expressions

An expression is a combination of values, variables, operators, and function calls that Python evaluates to produce a result.

Statements

A statement is an instruction that Python executes.

---

🔹 Python Strings

What is a String?

A string is a sequence of characters used to represent text in Python.

Example:

name = "Sameer"

Creating Strings

Strings can be created using different types of quotation marks.

Single Quotes

text = 'Hello'

Double Quotes

text = "Hello"

Triple Quotes

Triple quotes can be used for multi-line strings.

text = """Hello
Python
World"""

---

🔹 String Indexing

Indexing is used to access individual characters from a string.

Positive Indexing

Positive indexing starts from "0" from the left side.

Python
012345

Example:

text = "Python"
print(text[0])

Negative Indexing

Negative indexing starts from "-1" from the right side.

Python
-6 -5 -4 -3 -2 -1

Example:

text = "Python"
print(text[-1])

---

🔹 String Slicing

String slicing is used to extract a portion of a string.

Syntax:

string[start:stop:step]

Example:

text = "Python"
print(text[0:3])

---

🔹 String Concatenation

Concatenation combines two or more strings.

first = "Python"
second = "Programming"

result = first + " " + second
print(result)

---

🔹 String Repetition

The "*" operator can be used to repeat a string.

text = "Python "

print(text * 3)

---

🔹 String Length

The "len()" function returns the number of characters in a string.

text = "Python"

print(len(text))

---

🔹 String Immutability

Strings in Python are immutable, meaning the original string cannot be changed after it is created.

Instead of modifying the original string, Python creates a new string when a different value is assigned.

---

🔹 String Methods

"lower()"

Converts characters to lowercase.

text = "PYTHON"
print(text.lower())

"upper()"

Converts characters to uppercase.

text = "python"
print(text.upper())

"strip()"

Removes leading and trailing whitespace.

text = "  Python  "
print(text.strip())

"replace()"

Replaces one substring with another.

text = "I like Java"
print(text.replace("Java", "Python"))

"split()"

Splits a string into a list.

text = "Python is easy"
print(text.split())

"join()"

Joins elements of an iterable into a string.

words = ["Python", "is", "powerful"]

print(" ".join(words))

"find()"

Returns the position of the first occurrence of a specified substring.

text = "Python Programming"

print(text.find("Programming"))

